// Test: an interface-level selection is rewritten into ONE INLINE FRAGMENT PER
// IMPLEMENTING OBJECT as soon as ANY implementor is a client extension.
//
// This pins a scaling mechanism, not a resolver bug. `IThing` here has three
// implementors — one server type and one client model — so a single interface-level
// selection expands to one arm per implementor. (Deliberately two implementors, not
// three: with two symmetric server types the emitted arm ORDER is not stable across
// builds, which would make this fixture flaky. The count is what is being pinned.) The expansion is linear in the number
// of implementors and is triggered by the PRESENCE of a client implementor, not by
// the selection asking for anything client-specific.
//
// Why this is pinned at all: in a real internal schema `Node` has ~44k implementors,
// five of them client extensions — three belonging to an unrelated product that
// merely shares the schema. A single `id` through a `Node`-typed field expanded to
// 44,119 inline fragments, and a bare `nodes(ids: $ids) { id }` with no resolver
// anywhere in it expanded identically. Every `Node`-typed selection in that project
// pays it.
//
// That fan-out is what made `skip_redundant_nodes` recurse ~44k deep and kill the
// compiler with SIGSEGV. D118149581 fixed the TEARDOWN so the compiler survives and
// reports; it did not reduce the expansion. This fixture holds the expansion itself,
// so a future narrowing — expanding only for implementors the selection can actually
// reach, say — has to update it rather than pass silently.
//
// NOT caused by resolvers or by exec time: there is no `@exec_time_resolvers` here.
// `@waterfall` is required simply because this is the ordinary read-time shape for a
// client edge to an interface with server implementors; it is not what drives the
// expansion.

